PROJECT: AI-Generated Image Detector — Local MLOps Pipeline
VERSION: v0.1 Design Brief
SCOPE: Local deployment only. No cloud infra, no distributed training.

------------------------------------------------------------
1. OBJECTIVE
------------------------------------------------------------
Given a webpage URL, extract all images, classify each as
AI-generated vs real, return a confidence score. Wrapped in a
minimal MLOps loop (train -> track -> serve -> monitor) that
runs entirely on one machine (CPU or single GPU).

------------------------------------------------------------
2. DATA
------------------------------------------------------------
- Dataset: CIFAKE (real vs AI-generated, ~120k images, small)
- Source: HuggingFace `datasets` library (auto-caches locally)
- No manual download/hosting needed
- Split: use provided train/test split as-is for v0.1

------------------------------------------------------------
3. PREPROCESSING
------------------------------------------------------------
- Resize + normalize: torchvision.transforms
- (Optional v0.2) FFT-based frequency artifact feature via numpy.fft
- Keep preprocessing as a single pure function -> easy to unit test
  and reuse identically at train time and inference time

------------------------------------------------------------
4. MODEL
------------------------------------------------------------
- Backbone: CLIP image encoder (open_clip or transformers), frozen
- Head: scikit-learn LogisticRegression on CLIP embeddings
- Rationale: no GPU fine-tuning required, trains in seconds,
  strong baseline for synthetic image detection
- Fallback if accuracy insufficient: fine-tune timm efficientnet-b0

------------------------------------------------------------
5. TRAINING SCRIPT (train.py)
------------------------------------------------------------
Steps:
  1. Load CIFAKE via datasets
  2. Extract CLIP embeddings for train/test images
  3. Fit LogisticRegression on embeddings
  4. Evaluate (accuracy, F1, confusion matrix)
  5. Log run to MLflow (params, metrics, model artifact)
  6. Register model in local MLflow model registry if metrics
     beat current "production" tag

------------------------------------------------------------
6. EXPERIMENT TRACKING
------------------------------------------------------------
- Tool: MLflow, local file-store backend (no server process)
- Tracked: hyperparams, accuracy/F1, model artifact, dataset version tag
- UI: `mlflow ui` command, local browser access only

------------------------------------------------------------
7. WEBPAGE IMAGE EXTRACTION (scrape.py)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Static pages: requests + BeautifulSoup, pull all <img> src URLs
- JS-rendered pages: playwright (headless) as fallback if <img>
  count is suspiciously low
- Dedupe: imagehash (perceptual hash) before running inference
- Output: local temp folder of downloaded images + manifest.json
  (url, local path, hash)

------------------------------------------------------------
8. INFERENCE / SERVING (serve.py)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Load model: latest "production"-tagged model from MLflow registry
- Export path (optional, faster local inference): ONNX via onnxruntime
- API: FastAPI, single endpoint
    POST /analyze  { "url": "<webpage>" }
    -> runs scrape.py -> preprocess -> inference
    -> returns JSON: [{image_url, ai_probability, label}]
- Run locally: `uvicorn serve:app --reload`

------------------------------------------------------------
9. MONITORING (monitor.py, optional v0.2)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Tool: Evidently AI
- Track: embedding drift of incoming webpage images vs training
  distribution, prediction distribution over time
- Output: local HTML drift report, generated on-demand (not a
  live dashboard for v0.1)

------------------------------------------------------------
10. REPO STRUCTURE
------------------------------------------------------------
ai-image-detector/
  data/              # cached datasets (gitignored)
  models/            # exported ONNX / joblib artifacts
  mlruns/            # MLflow local tracking store
  src/
    preprocess.py    # shared transform function
    train.py         # training + MLflow logging
    scrape.py         # webpage -> image manifest
    serve.py          # FastAPI app
    monitor.py         # (v0.2) drift report
  requirements.txt
  README.md

------------------------------------------------------------
11. CORE DEPENDENCIES
------------------------------------------------------------
datasets, open_clip (or transformers), scikit-learn, mlflow,
beautifulsoup4, playwright, imagehash, onnxruntime, fastapi,
uvicorn, evidently (optional, v0.2)

------------------------------------------------------------
12. BUILD ORDER (for vibe coding)
------------------------------------------------------------
1. preprocess.py + train.py -> get a working local model + MLflow run
2. scrape.py -> confirm image extraction works on 2-3 test pages
3. serve.py -> wire trained model into FastAPI endpoint, test end-to-end
4. (only if needed) ONNX export for faster inference
5. (v0.2) monitor.py drift reporting

------------------------------------------------------------
13. OUT OF SCOPE (v0.1)
------------------------------------------------------------
- Cloud deployment, containers, CI/CD pipelines
- Distributed training, GPU clusters
- Live monitoring dashboards
- Multi-generator fine-grained classification (which model
  generated it) — binary real/AI only for now